      <div class="prose">
        <div class="abstract"><h2>Abstract</h2>
          <p>A lab collaboration in which I am fourth of eight authors. A passive index fund must hold everything in the benchmark; a conventional enhanced index fund is allowed small tilts but is held back by its own tracking-error mandate. N-EIF splits the problem in two. Phase 1 uses a genetic algorithm to pick and weight a basket of KOSPI 200 names that tracks the index. Phase 2 sits on top of that basket and decides when to be invested, using two signals that must agree: a <b>Volume-Price Index</b> (VPI), which measures whether the volume–price relationship has entered a stable, predictable regime, and an <b>LSTM dueling DQN</b> agent. Disagreement means hold. What distinguishes the paper from a single reported backtest is that it decomposes the result into the contribution of each of the three layers, twice — once through non-parametric rank tests and once through a panel fixed-effects regression — and the two decompositions agree.</p>
          <div class="kw"><b>Keywords</b> deep reinforcement learning · Volume-Price Index · genetic algorithm · enhanced indexing · portfolio optimization</div>
        </div>

        <div class="stats" style="margin-top:32px">
          <div class="stat"><div class="v">199<small>%</small></div><div class="l">Cumulative return, 2014–2025</div><div class="d">vs 84% for KOSPI 200, gross</div></div>
