        <h3 id="b2-1"><span class="num">2.1</span>Phase 1: the tracking basket</h3>
        <p>Following an earlier line of work from the same lab, stock selection is an iterative sector rotation. At each step the sector with the largest remaining market capitalisation is chosen, and within it a stock is picked by a priority score combining three terms: the inverse of the standard deviation of the estimated beta (so that names with a <em>stable</em> systematic risk profile are preferred), normalised trading volume, and normalised market capitalisation. The chosen stock is removed and the procedure repeats until the basket size <i>l</i> is reached; <i>l</i> ∈ &#123;20, 30, 50&#125; is swept.</p>
        <p>Weights are then optimised by the genetic algorithm against a variance-scaled tracking objective — deviations from benchmark weights are penalised more heavily in higher-variance names:</p>
        <div class="math">min<sub><i>w</i></sub> &nbsp; <i>Q</i>(<i>w</i><sub>1</sub>,…,<i>w</i><sub><i>l</i></sub>) = Σ<sub><i>k</i>=1..<i>l</i></sub> (<i>w</i><sub><i>k</i></sub> − <i>w</i><sup><i>m</i></sup><sub><i>k</i></sub>)<sup>2</sup> σ<sup>2</sup><sub><i>k</i></sub>,&nbsp;&nbsp; 0 ≤ <i>w</i><sub><i>k</i></sub> ≤ 1, &nbsp; Σ<sub><i>k</i></sub> <i>w</i><sub><i>k</i></sub> = 1<span class="eq">(1)</span></div>
        <p>with <i>w</i><sup><i>m</i></sup><sub><i>k</i></sub> the benchmark capitalisation weight. GA settings: population 50, adaptive crossover 0.5–0.8, adaptive mutation 0.05–0.06, terminating when the last 5,000 trials produce no improvement above 1%. The resulting portfolio, held passively, is reported throughout as the "Buy &amp; Hold (GA)" baseline — which is what makes the first layer's contribution isolable.</p>

        <h3 id="b2-2"><span class="num">2.2</span>Definition: the Volume-Price Index</h3>
        <p>The VPI is built in two stages. First, a local covariance between volume and price is computed over a moving window of length <i>q</i>:</p>
        <div class="math"><i>Y</i><sub><i>i</i></sub> = (1/<i>q</i>) Σ<sub><i>j</i>=<i>i</i>−<i>q</i>+1..<i>i</i></sub> (<i>V</i><sub><i>j</i></sub> − <i>V̄</i>)(<i>P</i><sub><i>j</i></sub> − <i>P̄</i>)<span class="eq">(2)</span></div>
        <p>Second, the VPI at time <i>t</i> is the lag-one autocorrelation of that series of local covariances, again over a window of <i>q</i>:</p>
        <div class="math">VPI<sub><i>t</i></sub> = corr( <i>Y</i><sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>Y</i><sub><i>i</i>−1</sub> ) over <i>i</i> = <i>t</i>−<i>q</i>+1 … <i>t</i><span class="eq">(3)</span></div>
        <p>The reading is the interpretive core of the paper. When |VPI| is near 1, successive local covariances barely change: the volume–price coupling has settled into one regime, and the paper calls this a <em>variation-decrease moment</em> — a window in which the market is directionally more predictable. A low VPI means the coupling is oscillating between regimes, and the prescribed action is to hold. Note what is being measured: not the direction of the market, but the stability of a relationship. Direction comes from elsewhere.</p>
        <p>Signals fire only on a crossing, not on a level: VPI must rise above a threshold <i>p</i><sub>0</sub> from below, and the direction is then taken from recent price action — buy on two or more increases in the last three trading days, sell on two or more decreases. The threshold <i>p</i><sub>0</sub> is set by grid search over percentiles of the VPI distribution during training, choosing the percentile with the highest training return, and is recalibrated every 1, 10 or 30 days depending on configuration.</p>
        <p>Behind the construction sits an asymmetry argument: volume responds more strongly to positive price changes than to negative ones, which the paper attributes to information-driven buying and, in the Korean market specifically, to short-selling restrictions that suppress sell-side volume. A figure on KOSPI 200 data reports an asymmetry ratio of 0.83 in support of this, though the manuscript does not define precisely which two quantities that ratio compares.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/projects/index-fund-hybrid/media/asymmetry.png" alt="Normalised volume against daily price change on KOSPI 200, split into up days and down days with separate fitted trends" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> That figure. Each point is one trading day of the KOSPI 200 over 2014-01 to 2025-09: horizontal axis the price change, vertical axis normalised volume, red for the 1,466 up days and blue for the 1,303 down days, with a separate trend fitted to each side. The claim is the difference in the two slopes, +5.576 against −6.701, printed as a ratio of 0.83. What a reader should also see is the scatter: the cloud is wide relative to either slope, so the asymmetry is a property of the fitted lines rather than something visible day by day — which is consistent with the paper's own position that the VPI is a regime measure and not a directional signal.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3 id="b2-3"><span class="num">2.3</span>The agent, and the consensus gate</h3>
        <p>An LSTM processes the sequence of market state, and its hidden state is the state vector handed to a dueling DQN, which splits the action-value estimate into a state-value stream and an advantage stream recombined in the usual way:</p>
        <div class="math"><i>Q</i>(<i>s</i>,<i>a</i>) = <i>V</i>(<i>s</i>) + [ <i>A</i>(<i>s</i>,<i>a</i>) − (1/|𝒜|) Σ<sub><i>a</i>′</sub> <i>A</i>(<i>s</i>,<i>a</i>′) ]<span class="eq">(4)</span></div>
        <p>Actions are chosen ε-greedily with ε decayed over training; learning uses experience replay and a target network. The reward is a weighted combination of realised return, a Sharpe term and a drawdown penalty, so the agent is not optimising raw return alone.</p>
        <p>The gate is deliberately conservative: a trade executes only when the rule-based VPI signal and the learned action agree on a non-hold direction. Any conflict produces a hold. Since the framework is long-only, a sell or a short resolves to cash rather than to a short position — a choice the paper justifies on grounds of capital efficiency and regulatory compatibility rather than performance.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/projects/index-fund-hybrid/media/consensus-gate.png" alt="Two-by-two table of VPI signal against LSTM-DQN signal, giving long, short or hold" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> The gate as the manuscript draws it: the agent's buy/sell on one axis, the VPI's buy/sell on the other, and the resulting action in the four cells. Only the two agreeing corners produce a position; both disagreeing corners produce a hold. Read as a filter this is severe — a signal that is right on its own is discarded whenever the other one differs — and §4.2 is where that severity gets priced: it is exactly what turns the standalone agent, the worst strategy in the study, into the best one.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="b3"><span class="num">3</span>Experiments</h2>
        <h3 id="b3-1"><span class="num">3.1</span>Setup</h3>
        <p>KOSPI 200 over 2014-01 to 2025-09, from the Korea Exchange, covering bull markets, corrections and the COVID-19 period. The index serves as both benchmark and investment universe; sector diversification uses the WICS classification at its 26-sector granularity. Two retraining regimes are run in parallel to test sensitivity to adaptation frequency: retrain every three months and test on the following two, or retrain every six months and test on the following one.</p>
        <p>Five strategies are compared, and they are nested so that the differences are interpretable: the KOSPI 200 benchmark; Buy &amp; Hold GA (Phase 1 only); VPI Rule-Based (Phase 1 + VPI timing, no learning); standalone LSTM-DQN (learning with neither GA selection nor VPI gating); and the full N-EIF.</p>
        <h3 id="b3-2"><span class="num">3.2</span>Hyperparameters</h3>
        <p>An ablation over 3<sup>5</sup> = 243 configurations: VPI window &#123;10, 20, 30&#125; days, momentum lookback &#123;3, 5, 7&#125; days, basket size &#123;20, 30, 50&#125;, rebalancing frequency &#123;1, 2, 3&#125; months, and <i>p</i><sub>0</sub> recalibration interval &#123;1, 10, 30&#125; days. Screening found the first three to drive 30–50% swings in total return while the last two moved it by under 5%, so the last two were fixed at their best values and the remaining 27 combinations examined in full. Both retraining regimes converged on the same three: VPI window 10 days, momentum lookback 3 days, basket 50.</p>
        <h3 id="b3-3"><span class="num">3.3</span>How the claims are tested</h3>
        <p>This is where the paper spends most of its effort, and it is the most defensible part of it. Shapiro–Wilk and Levene tests reject normality and equal variances, so the primary comparison is a non-parametric Friedman test across 134 monthly (21-trading-day) test periods, followed by pairwise Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with a Bonferroni correction at α = 0.05/4. Because cumulative returns are temporally dependent, a panel fixed-effects regression is run as a second, parametric route: cumulative return of strategy <i>i</i> at time <i>t</i> is a strategy-specific fixed effect plus a common linear time trend, with Newey–West HAC standard errors. The point of running both is that they rest on different assumptions and can therefore corroborate or contradict each other.</p>

        <h2 id="b4"><span class="num">4</span>Results</h2>
        <h3 id="b4-1"><span class="num">4.1</span>The five strategies</h3>
        <div class="twrap"><table>
