        <p class="cap"><b>Table 1.</b> Gross of costs, 2014–2025. The six-month retraining regime gives almost the same picture (N-EIF 196.96%, Sharpe 0.807), so the result is not sensitive to adaptation frequency. Note the ordering of the drawdown column: it falls monotonically as layers are added.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/projects/index-fund-hybrid/media/cumulative-return.png" alt="Cumulative return paths of the five strategies on KOSPI 200 from 2014 to 2025, indexed to 100" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Table 1 as paths, indexed to 100 — the five legend figures are the table's first column. What the table cannot show is where the layers actually separate. Until 2017 the five lines are on top of each other; the GA basket pulls away from the index through 2017–2019; and the full framework only separates from the VPI rule after the March 2020 crash, holding a gap of roughly twenty index points from 2021 onward. The drawdown claim is visible at the same spot: every line collapses in February 2020, but the two gated strategies come back fastest. The red line is the standalone agent, below the benchmark for essentially the whole decade.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>The standalone agent is the row that carries the paper's most interesting finding. Left to itself, with neither GA selection nor VPI gating, the LSTM-DQN underperforms the passive index it was supposed to beat.</p>

        <h3 id="b4-2"><span class="num">4.2</span>Two independent decompositions that agree</h3>
        <p>The Friedman test across 134 test periods rejects equality decisively (χ² = 485.58, df = 4, p &lt; 0.001), with average ranks of 1.35 for N-EIF, 1.67 for the rule, 3.11 for the GA basket, 3.96 for the index and 4.91 for the standalone agent. The six-month regime gives the same ordering. Post-hoc Wilcoxon tests with the Bonferroni correction put N-EIF ahead of every other strategy at the corrected threshold.</p>
        <p>The panel regression then re-derives the same hierarchy from a different direction, after stripping out a common market trend of about 0.70% per month:</p>
        <div class="twrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Comparison</th><th class="n">Fixed-effect gap</th><th class="n">Mean-return gap</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>GA selection</td><td>Buy &amp; Hold GA vs KOSPI 200</td><td class="n">+20.46 pp</td><td class="n">+20.45 pp</td></tr>
            <tr><td>VPI gating</td><td>VPI Rule-Based vs Buy &amp; Hold GA</td><td class="n">+20.37 pp</td><td class="n">+20.38 pp</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Learned agent</td><td>N-EIF vs VPI Rule-Based</td><td class="n">+6.67 pp</td><td class="n">+6.67 pp</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td>VPI as a gate on the agent</td><td>N-EIF vs LSTM-DQN alone</td><td class="n">+62.26 pp</td><td class="n">+62.27 pp</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
        <p class="cap"><b>Table 2.</b> The decomposition, from the fixed-effects estimates and from raw mean cumulative returns over the 134 test periods. The two columns were produced by different methods and land within 0.01 of each other.</p>
        <figure>
          <img src="/projects/index-fund-hybrid/media/decomposition.png" alt="Waterfall of panel-regression fixed effects from the benchmark through the GA basket and the VPI rule to N-EIF" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Table 2's first column drawn as a waterfall of the panel-regression fixed effects, in percentage points relative to the common time trend. The benchmark sits at −25.56, the GA basket at −5.10, the VPI rule at +15.27 and the full framework at +21.94; the arrows are the three increments, +20.46 for selection, +20.37 for the gate and +6.67 for the learned agent. The proportions are the argument: the two structural layers are near-identical steps and the reinforcement-learning layer is roughly a third of either. Note what this chart leaves out — the standalone agent, whose fixed effect of −40.32 is off the bottom of the axis. Its absence is why the +62 point figure in the table has no bar here.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Read as a whole the decomposition says something a single headline number would hide. The two <em>structural</em> layers are worth about the same, roughly +20 points each. The learned agent adds +6.67 points on top of the rule — real, statistically resolved, and far smaller than the framing "deep reinforcement learning for trading" would suggest. And the largest number in the table is not an achievement of the agent at all: it is the cost of removing the gate from it. The agent's own fixed effect is −40.32 points, well below the index; gating turns the worst strategy in the study into the best one. The authors' framing is that VPI filtering lets the agent concentrate on timing instead of selection; a blunter reading is that most of what the agent produces on its own is noise, and the gate suppresses it.</p>
        <p>The risk-adjusted view agrees on the top of the ranking — monthly Sharpe ratios of 0.242, 0.215, 0.155, 0.129 and 0.081 — though this table orders the middle differently from the annualised Sharpe of Table 1, placing the standalone agent above the GA basket. The manuscript does not reconcile the two orderings.</p>

        <h3 id="b4-3"><span class="num">4.3</span>Model diagnostics</h3>
        <p>The panel model explains about 75% of the variation in cumulative returns (R² = 0.7487; within 0.6281, between 0.7127), and an F-test rejects equal fixed effects across strategies (F = 214.11). The paper also reports what did <em>not</em> work: a Ljung–Box test finds residual autocorrelation at every lag, so a single linear time trend does not capture the temporal structure. Clustered standard errors are used in response, and the paper concedes that this mitigates rather than removes the problem.</p>

        <h2 id="b5"><span class="num">5</span>Conclusion &amp; future work</h2>
        <h3 id="b5-1"><span class="num">5.1</span>What the paper does not establish</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Hyperparameters were selected on the evaluation period.</b> All 243 configurations are scored by total cumulative return over the same span the headline numbers come from, and the winner is then reported on that span. There is no out-of-sample period untouched by that search, so 199.46% is an upper bound on what the configuration would have produced in advance.</li>
          <li><b>Returns are gross.</b> The paper says so, and offers an estimate: at 0.2% per trade with roughly 12 trades a year, the net cumulative return would be around 175%. But the trade count is nowhere reported, and a framework making daily consensus decisions with <i>p</i><sub>0</sub> recalibrated as often as daily is not obviously a twelve-trade-a-year strategy. The net figure should be read as an illustration, not a measurement.</li>
          <li><b>Liquidity and market impact are absent</b>, as is any treatment of the cost of moving in and out of cash on the whole basket at once.</li>
          <li><b>The VPI's own economic content is asserted more than tested.</b> There is no test that a variation-decrease moment predicts anything on its own; its value is only ever observed through the full strategy's return.</li>
          <li><b>One market, one index, one currency.</b> Every result is KOSPI 200, and the short-selling-restriction argument behind the asymmetry is specific to that market.</li>
          <li><b>Two reporting slips a reader should notice.</b> The Buy &amp; Hold GA fixed effect is printed as t = −2.57 with p &lt; 0.001, which those two values cannot both be. And several cross-references point at the wrong sections — the ablation study is referred to as Section 4.1 when it is in Section 5, and the statistical tests as Section 4.2 when they are in Section 6.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="b5-2"><span class="num">5.2</span>Next</h3>
        <div class="take">
          <div><h4>What held up</h4><ul><li>The hierarchy is monotone in every metric reported — return, Sharpe and drawdown all improve as layers are added.</li><li>Two statistical routes with different assumptions produced the same decomposition to within 0.01 points.</li><li>The negative result on the standalone agent is reported rather than hidden, and it is the paper's most informative finding.</li></ul></div>
          <div><h4>Next</h4><ul><li>Choose the next venue after the ESWA decline</li><li>A genuine out-of-sample period for the hyperparameter choice</li><li>Trade counts and costs inside the optimisation, not estimated after it</li><li>Other markets, and variations on the VPI window and lag structure</li><li>Attribution on the agent (SHAP, attention) to see what it is gated on</li></ul></div>
        </div>

